## What You've Learnt

After completing this lesson, you can:

- ✓ Define RAM variables using EQU
- ✓ Store values to memory with LD (addr),A
- ✓ Load values from memory with LD A,(addr)
- ✓ Understand direction encoding (0-3)
- ✓ Know safe memory addresses for variables ($5B00+)
- ✓ Understand why registers aren't enough (temporary)
- ✓ Use memory to preserve state across subroutine calls
- ✓ Test values with CP and conditional jumps
- ✓ Organise code with labels and subroutines
- ✓ Build foundation for game state management

**Key insight:** Registers are workspace (temporary), RAM is storage (persistent). Games need both. Fast calculations happen in registers, results get saved to RAM.

**Critical understanding:** Every subroutine call can overwrite registers. If you need a value to survive, store it in RAM before calling.

**Next lesson:** Making It Move - Use your direction variable to actually move a dot around the screen! Learn 16-bit arithmetic (INC HL/DEC HL) and screen address calculation. The proto-snake begins!

